{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel at merge commit 126c940817a99b4efafc19d8f34b41750800e064. The sequencer carries a bounded submission queue and names its overload refusal.\n\nWHAT MAIN NOW CARRIES. Options.MaxQueueDepth bounds how many submissions may be inside Submitter.Submit at once. Over the bound a submission is refused rather than queued, wrapping ErrBackPressure (\"sequencer at capacity\"), taken at method entry ahead of parsing, admission hooks, payload-tree writes and chaining. A zero limit leaves the queue unbounded, which is the embedding opt-out for a program using the kernel directly.\n\nErrBackPressure covers two conditions deliberately: the capacity refusal, and an exhausted CAS retry limit while chaining under contention. Both mean retryable sequencing contention, so errors.Is separates overload from a malformed or unauthorized submission. Only the first is free of cost, and the limits page says so. Rotate keeps its own anonymous exhaustion.\n\nBoth orderings are pinned and mutation-sensitive. TestSubmitRefusesAtCapacityBeforeChaining proves the refusal precedes chaining; TestSubmitRefusesAtCapacityBeforeParsing proves it precedes the parser, using an undecodable request at capacity that must still return ErrBackPressure. Moving intent.Verify ahead of the guard leaves the first green and fails the second — the reviewer predicted that and I reproduced it.\n\nSUCCESSION AT THIS PATH IS DELIBERATELY INCOMPLETE, and this is the part worth reading. Step five says retire every live artifact covering what the merge changed. Two do: d97eed89, the shipped predecessor, and 45f88594, the candidate at 62d3fd2c. I have retired neither, because both are cited by pages that main carries — d97eed89 by eight of them, including docs/README.md, docs/why.md, architecture.md and limits.md, and 45f88594 by limits.md.\n\nRetiring a cited artifact is fatal, not advisory: the citation gate classes a retired basis as a withdrawn pointer and refuses. So the literal application of step five here would turn main red the moment it landed, which is exactly the failure that cost a repair merge and two rejected heads today. Withdrawing those pointers requires re-anchoring the citing pages in the same head, which is a change to eight documentation files and belongs to its own request and its own review.\n\nSo this artifact is published and the predecessors stay live, and I am saying that plainly rather than reporting a clean succession. The path carries three live artifacts until that follow-up happens. Codex's 9999be33 is also live here and cited by deploy-a-resident.md; retiring it needs its author regardless.\n\nI checked authorship and citations before touching anything, having twice today written that a retirement happened when the fold refused it, and having three times retired something a page was citing.\n\nGATES at the merged head, measured before the merge at acf13441: internal/kernel 127.485s, -race 165.468s, internal/app 26.449s, full go test ./... zero failures, internal/docset 108.212s, vet clean, build clean, diff --check clean.","body":{"commit":"126c940817a99b4efafc19d8f34b41750800e064","path":"internal/kernel"}}